    {
      "location": "lib/core/navigation/placeholder_screens.dart:58-70 (BiometricReauthPlaceholderScreen, unchanged from c35f704 through current HEAD)",
      "trigger_condition": "The expiry-overlay's only call-to-action ('Entrar novamente' in expiry_overlay_host.dart:125, `widget.router.go(AppRoutePaths.reauth)`) lands on a screen that renders only a centered 'Biometria' Text with no AppBar, no back arrow, and no button — unlike every sibling placeholder (Meus Registros/Planos de Ação/Configurações), which all reuse `_BackArrowHeader` to get back Home.",
      "guard_snippet": "Give BiometricReauthPlaceholderScreen the same `_BackArrowHeader`-style escape hatch the other placeholders use (or at minimum a visible way back to `/auth`/`/home`), until Epic 2's real biometric/PIN flow replaces it.",
      "potential_consequence": "Any user whose session actually expires mid-use and taps the overlay's only button is stranded on a dead screen with zero interactive elements — the one real, reachable user flow this story ships leads straight into a trap."
    },
    {
      "location": "lib/core/network/retry_interceptor.dart:74-77 (shouldRetry, as of c35f704 — the end of this epic's diff; a non-idempotent-function exclusion list was only added much later, in story 6.1)",
      "trigger_condition": "`shouldRetry` retries purely on {error code is one of the 6 retryable codes, attempt < maxRetries} — it has no concept of 'this POST has no idempotency key and already had an effect.' `dio_error_code.dart` can yield NETWORK_TIMEOUT/NETWORK_OFFLINE from `DioException.type` alone, before any response body exists, so this fires even when the original request's effect already landed server-side. `auth-signup`, `auth-login` and `auth-refresh` (all plain POSTs, no idempotency key) are retried exactly like any other call.",
      "guard_snippet": "Exclude `auth-signup`/`auth-login`/`auth-refresh` from automatic retry the same way a later commit eventually excludes the chat/export writes — or, better, settle this exclusion list in Story 1.1 itself rather than leaving every auth write unprotected through the end of the epic.",
      "potential_consequence": "A signup whose response is lost to a timeout gets silently replayed with the identical email/password; the backend rejects the duplicate account and a successful signup surfaces to the user as a failure. Worse: `auth-refresh` is a 'thin GoTrue proxy' (openapi.yaml:177) where refresh tokens are typically single-use/rotating — a timed-out-but-actually-successful refresh gets replayed with the now-already-consumed token, the backend returns AUTH_INVALID, and `SessionRepositoryImpl.restoreSession()` (session_repository_impl.dart:148-156) treats that as a backend-confirmed-dead token and clears the stored session — logging out a user whose session was in fact still valid."
    },
    {
      "location": "lib/core/network/dio_error_code.dart:19-32 (unchanged since 738bf17, the last commit to touch this file)",
      "trigger_condition": "Any `DioException` whose `type` is NOT connectionTimeout/sendTimeout/receiveTimeout/cancel and whose `response` is null — e.g. `DioExceptionType.badCertificate`, or `DioExceptionType.unknown` from a malformed request — falls through the switch's `default: break` and lands on `if (e.response == null) return 'NETWORK_OFFLINE'`, one of RetryInterceptor's 6 retryable codes.",
      "guard_snippet": "Give `badCertificate` (and any other non-timeout, no-response type) its own non-retryable mapping instead of folding it into NETWORK_OFFLINE, e.g. map it straight to a non-retryable malformedResponse/distinct failure.",
      "potential_consequence": "A deterministic TLS/certificate failure — which will fail identically on every attempt — gets retried twice with the fixed 500ms delay before surfacing, and is then reported to the user with the exact same 'you appear offline' messaging as a real connectivity gap, burying a security-relevant signal (bad cert, intercepting proxy, clock skew) under a generic offline banner."
    },
    {
      "location": "lib/features/auth/auth_injection.dart:35-40 vs 46-48 (c35f704)",
      "trigger_condition": "`SessionExpiryNotifier` and `SessionRepository` are both registered with an explicit `dispose:` callback so `sl.reset()` closes their `StreamController`s; `AuthCubit` — itself a `Cubit` wrapping its own internal `StreamController` — is registered with none.",
      "guard_snippet": "`sl.registerLazySingleton<AuthCubit>(() => AuthCubit(sl(), sl<SessionRepository>()), dispose: (cubit) => cubit.close());`",
      "potential_consequence": "Every `sl.reset()` call — which is every test file's teardown via `resetCoreDependencies()` — leaks one unclosed Cubit instead of releasing it, silently drifting from the project's own documented DI-lifecycle convention as more features accumulate their own cubits."
    },
